Parent: 1415

## Context
The author's description (2026-11-01): Imagine can be interactive, working
towards a design note in design/ on the standard plan (problem; situation
from git; state of the art from docs, web and forums; alternative solutions,
the most direct one and one from a different family; synthesis and
recommendation with cost estimates; phased implementation plan; double
advisor-level review plus KISS), now written as
design/design-note-template.md. It can also be agentic, as raid's Phase 2,
whose past use the author doubts was constructive.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] A skill drives the template with the author section by section, then the double advisor review and the KISS pass
- [ ] raid Phase 2 delegates to it per ticket instead of its inline prompt
- [ ] An audit of past raid logs records what Phase 2 actually produced
- [ ] One real design note produced with the skill
- [ ] The catalog entry fits in the room made by 1416

## Imagine (raid 1f0534)
- Raid 1f0534 executes only exit criterion 3 (audit of past raid logs: what Phase 2 produced); the skill itself is interactive with the author and waits. Not closed by this raid.

## Feasibility (raid 1f0534)
- PASS (audit only). Exclude raid 1f0534's own Imagine sections from the sample.

## De-risk (2026-11-09)

Session reading against design/2026-11-01-raid-phase2-imagine-audit.md
(moved from conception/ by ticket 1477) and the raid journals of
2026-11-07 to 2026-11-09; one Blind-Spot seat (opus, same family) read the
draft and its findings are folded in. Judgments are the session's.

1. Criterion 4 first, on the bare template. Since the template landed on
   2026-11-01 no note in design/ follows it, and nothing shows a session
   skipped it for want of a skill. One real note written with the author on
   the template, before any skill, measures what a skill must add
   (sequencing, state-of-the-art search, review launch, KISS pass) and may
   shrink this ticket to criteria 3 and 4. Candidate: 1443 (memory redesign;
   the author's design input of 2026-11-08 waits for a session of this
   kind). 1369 stays closed to implementation until the author reopens it.
2. One skill, two callers. Criterion 1 (interactive: a question in, a note
   out, the author in the loop) and criterion 2 (agentic: a ticket in, an
   annotation out) share a name and little else: inputs, outputs, cost,
   runtime constraints. Built together the ticket trips the monster signals
   (SKILL.md and references, raid SKILL.md, rules/workflow.md § Cycle,
   catalog, tests, the note). Criterion 2 becomes a child of 1415 once the
   author rules on item 3.
3. Decision for the author: raid Phase 2's per-ticket reimagine. Evidence:
   the audit (15 of 45 annotations claimed a premise or scope change;
   delivery unmeasured; "zero criteria edited" says nothing, the drift guard
   forbids such edits by design). After the audit: raid 829e9e's per-ticket
   Imagine (opus) scoped a partial prune correctly; raid 632b19's (sonnet)
   said "no script edits needed" and was wrong, the Blind-Spot pass caught
   it; raids 88e70d and 1479 skipped the per-ticket agents after the author
   pre-ruled, and the Blind-Spot pass still caught a stranded exit check.
   Options: (a) keep the per-ticket reimagine and delegate it to the skill;
   (b) cut it to hunt § Plan's premise check plus the Blind-Spot pass;
   (c) leave Phase 2 as written; (d) write down current practice: the
   Blind-Spot pass always, the per-ticket reimagine at the lead's judgment,
   a stronger worker when it runs. Session default: (d); it costs one
   paragraph in raid and no agentic skill mode.
4. Pointer sweep. The phase is named in rules/workflow.md:19 and 35-38
   (§ Posture, § Cycle), skills/hunt/references/session-start.md:12
   (explore-* opens in Imagine: the skill's natural entry),
   skills/verify-adherence/SKILL.md:45,
   skills/harness-doctor/references/toolkit-diagnostics.md:256. A skill
   landing sweeps the unit in one commit (workflow § Autonomous action).
   Resident cap 15100 chars, comments stripped
   (tests/test_rules_resident_budget.py): the skill name replaces the two
   § Cycle pointers; measure with scripts/resident_census.py in the PR.
5. Criterion 5 is unverifiable as written: 1416 made no room (closed moot;
   5330 model-invocable description chars on Claude Code). Proposed
   restatement, the number the session's own: description under 160 chars
   including routing hints; `make skills-catalog` and
   `make check-skills-drift` pass.
6. Review cost per run. Template § Review asks two decorrelated
   advisor-level reviews plus KISS; a cross-family seat costs money and
   consent (gaze_seat.py consent, cross_family_spend) and an advisor call is
   never a standing setting (raid § Model policy). The skill scales the
   review to the stakes (decorrelation.md table), states the spend before
   launch, records "same family, not independent"; two seats by default
   only for a note that changes rules, guards or gates. Also:
   rules/workflow.md:19 asks for options with probabilities and prices,
   template § 5 for costs only; the skill or the template settles which wins.
7. Three runtimes. Codex and Pi have no question widget: ask in plain
   conversation, write the note section by section, nothing
   runtime-specific outside a harness-extension-point
   (scripts/check-agnostic.sh). Unattended (raid, a background job) the
   skill degrades to the template's "draft alone, mark every judgment as
   the agent's"; it does not fail.
8. Name. Dragon names stay; a later rename costs path pointers (1416
   counted about ten). Default `imagine`, the phase's name in every rule;
   the author decides before the branch opens.
9. Criterion 3 is done with the box open: the audit exists with stated
   limits (one reader, no A/B). Tick on the author's acceptance, or add a
   second reader.

Order: author rulings (3, 8) → the 1443 note on the bare template (1) →
a skill only for what the note showed missing (criteria 1, 5) → a Phase 2
child only under (a).
